WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:17.828
Nemo and I returned to the gallery, where we settled ourselves on one of the couches.

2
00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:20.550
He showed me a set of drawings of the Nautilus.

3
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:27.296
"As you can see, Professor Aronnax," said the captain, "my ship is shaped like a cigar, measuring 232

4
00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:28.797
feet in length and 26 feet at its widest point.

5
00:00:32.744 --> 00:00:38.008
It has two steel hulls joined together, which make it as strong as if it were made of

6
00:00:38.128 --> 00:00:38.708
solid metal."

7
00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:41.010
"How does it sink under the water?"

8
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.971
I asked, studying the plans.

9
00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:49.015
"The submarine has reservoirs that can be filled with seawater when we need to descend," he explained.

10
00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:54.199
"But we don't have to take on a huge amount of water because everything has a natural tendency

11
00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:54.999
to sink in water."

12
00:00:55.019 --> 00:00:58.441
"Then how do you make the submarine rise to the surface?"

13
00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:02.797
"All we have to do is pump out the extra water," said the captain.

14
00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:07.100
"The pumps, which run on electricity, are very powerful.

15
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.402
But I'm sure you observed that when jets of water burst over the Abraham Lincoln."

16
00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:13.804
I remembered that quite well.

17
00:01:13.824 --> 00:01:19.748
"The submarine also has rudders on either side that help to guide it up or down," the captain

18
00:01:19.768 --> 00:01:20.088
went on.

19
00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:22.970
"But how can the pilot see where he's going?"

20
00:01:23.431 --> 00:01:23.851
I asked.

21
00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:27.534
"He sits in a glass pilot house on top of the vessel.

22
00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:30.836
Behind him is a powerful electric light that shows him the way."

23
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:32.980
"Aha!" I said.

24
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.101
"That explains the glow of the 'narwhal' that puzzled us so.

25
00:01:37.502 --> 00:01:41.002
Well, Captain, your Nautilus is certainly a marvelous vessel."

26
00:01:41.023 --> 00:01:46.144
"It is indeed, Professor, and I love it as if it were a part of myself.

27
00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:50.405
I am the engineer, builder, and captain of this wonderful boat."

28
00:01:50.425 --> 00:01:54.186
"But how did you manage to build it in secret?"

29
00:01:54.566 --> 00:01:54.946
I asked.

30
00:01:55.846 --> 00:01:58.767
"Each part came from a different place," said Captain Nemo.

31
00:01:58.787 --> 00:02:06.003
"The keel was made in France, the propeller in Scotland, the engine in Prussia, and the instruments in

32
00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:06.463
New York.

33
00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:11.627
I gave a different name to every supplier, so that no one could trace the orders back to

34
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:18.111
me." "Yes, but someone had to put the various parts together," I said, still mystified.

35
00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:21.094
"I set up my workshop on a desert island.

36
00:02:21.714 --> 00:02:27.138
There my crew, whom I had trained, put the Nautilus together under my direction.

37
00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:30.160
When we finished, we burned all traces of our work."

38
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.325
I couldn't begin to imagine how much this vessel was worth, especially considering the value of the treasures

39
00:02:36.405 --> 00:02:40.229
onboard. "One last question, Captain Nemo," I said.

40
00:02:40.249 --> 00:02:41.550
"Are you rich?"

41
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.052
"Immensely rich, Professor.

42
00:02:44.472 --> 00:02:47.315
I could pay the national debt of France and never miss the money."

43
00:02:48.816 --> 00:02:49.657
I stared at him.

44
00:02:50.458 --> 00:02:51.419
Was he pulling my leg?

45
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.100
Time would tell.

46
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.704
"Now," said Captain Nemo, "it's almost noon.

47
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.441
Time for us to figure out our location and determine our starting point for this voyage.

48
00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:03.282
We need to go back up to the surface."

49
00:03:04.262 --> 00:03:06.203
The captain pressed a button three times.

50
00:03:07.163 --> 00:03:11.924
The pumps began to drive the water from the tanks; the needle on one of his instruments showed

51
00:03:11.944 --> 00:03:13.365
that we were rising to the surface.

52
00:03:14.325 --> 00:03:15.345
The needle stopped moving.

53
00:03:15.365 --> 00:03:21.407
"We've arrived," said the captain, who then led the way to a platform on top of the submarine.

54
00:03:22.407 --> 00:03:24.808
The platform was only three feet above the water.

55
00:03:25.632 --> 00:03:30.397
Toward the middle of the platform I saw the slight bump that indicated where the small boat was

56
00:03:30.457 --> 00:03:38.285
stored. I also observed the two glass enclosures that the captain had described, one for the pilot and

57
00:03:38.305 --> 00:03:40.487
the other for the electric light that guided him.

58
00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:44.491
The sea was beautiful and the sky was clear.

59
00:03:45.252 --> 00:03:47.414
A light breeze rippled the surface of the water.

60
00:03:48.155 --> 00:03:49.116
There was nothing in sight.

61
00:03:50.068 --> 00:03:55.430
Captain Nemo used his sextant to measure the altitude of the sun, thus determining our latitude.

62
00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.870
"It's noon," said the captain.

63
00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:03.492
"Professor, whenever you're ready He indicated the hatch that led to the submarine's interior.

64
00:04:04.492 --> 00:04:07.473
I took one last look at the sea and climbed down the ladder.

65
00:04:08.313 --> 00:04:10.234
I felt the vessel beginning to descend.

66
00:04:11.234 --> 00:04:17.876
In the gallery Captain Nemo used another instrument to calculate our longitude and determine our exact position on

67
00:04:17.916 --> 00:04:18.196
the sea.

68
00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:24.124
"Our voyage under the seas began at noon today, November 8," he said.

69
00:04:24.144 --> 00:04:28.888
"We are headed northeast at a depth of 156 feet.

70
00:04:29.249 --> 00:04:31.551
Here are some maps upon which you can follow our course."

71
00:04:31.931 --> 00:04:32.552
He bowed to me.

72
00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:35.614
"And now I'll leave you to your studies."

73
00:04:36.655 --> 00:04:40.318
I spent the next hour thinking about Captain Nemo and his amazing submarine.

74
00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:45.623
Then I noticed a large map on the table, and I put my finger on the spot where

75
00:04:45.663 --> 00:04:46.604
our voyage had begun.

76
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.424
I realized that we were following the Black River, a powerful ocean current like the Gulf Stream.

77
00:04:53.505 --> 00:04:58.269
I was still staring at the map when Ned and Conseil appeared at the door of the gallery.

78
00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:03.133
They were standing still, frozen at the sight of the wonders spread before them.

79
00:05:03.153 --> 00:05:05.194
"Where are we?"

80
00:05:05.615 --> 00:05:06.135
cried Ned.

81
00:05:06.155 --> 00:05:08.377
"In a museum in Quebec?"

82
00:05:09.418 --> 00:05:13.121
"No, it must be a museum in Paris," insisted Conseil.

83
00:05:14.142 --> 00:05:15.383
I motioned for them to enter.

84
00:05:15.403 --> 00:05:24.583
"My friends, you are in neither Canada nor France but onboard the Nautilus, 156 feet below sea level."

85
00:05:25.463 --> 00:05:29.925
"Professor Aronnax," said Ned, "can you tell me how many men are onboard with us?

86
00:05:30.285 --> 00:05:32.846
Ten, twenty, fifty, a hundred?"

87
00:05:32.866 --> 00:05:35.367
"I don't know," I replied.

88
00:05:35.387 --> 00:05:40.529
"I think you should give up any idea of seizing the Nautilus or escaping from it right now.

89
00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:46.512
This ship is a masterpiece of modern industry, and I'm glad I have the chance to see it.

90
00:05:46.856 --> 00:05:49.637
So be quiet for a while and let's see what happens."

91
00:05:50.777 --> 00:05:52.158
"See!" exclaimed Ned.

92
00:05:52.178 --> 00:05:55.078
"But we can see nothing in this underwater prison!

93
00:05:55.398 --> 00:05:56.599
We're sailing blindly!"

94
00:05:57.579 --> 00:06:00.940
Ned had barely said these words when all the lights went out.

95
00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.201
The three of us didn't dare to speak; we didn't know what would happen next.

96
00:06:06.201 --> 00:06:08.762
We heard something that sounded like panels sliding.

97
00:06:08.782 --> 00:06:10.303
"It's the end!"

98
00:06:10.723 --> 00:06:11.263
cried Ned.

99
00:06:12.263 --> 00:06:17.133
Suddenly light appeared at either end of the gallery, thanks to two large windows.

100
00:06:18.093 --> 00:06:21.615
The sea was clearly visible for a mile around the Nautilus.

101
00:06:22.476 --> 00:06:23.937
What a spectacle!

102
00:06:23.957 --> 00:06:27.679
"Well, you wished to see, Ned," I said.

103
00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:29.580
"Now you can see."

104
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.781
"Amazing! Truly amazing!"

105
00:06:33.221 --> 00:06:35.503
muttered the harpooner, forgetting his bad mood.

106
00:06:36.923 --> 00:06:39.925
Conseil was equally captivated by the view through the windows.

107
00:06:40.906 --> 00:06:45.268
As Ned named the fish, Conseil put them into their scientific classifications.

108
00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:51.662
I won't mention all the varieties that passed before my dazzled eyes for two hours, but they represented

109
00:06:51.762 --> 00:06:55.344
a superb collection of specimens that I had only read about.

110
00:06:56.304 --> 00:07:02.347
These fish, more numerous than the birds of the air, were undoubtedly attracted by the brilliance of the

111
00:07:02.407 --> 00:07:03.767
submarine's electric light.

112
00:07:04.708 --> 00:07:10.070
Suddenly there was light in the gallery, the panels closed, and the enchanting vision disappeared.

113
00:07:11.071 --> 00:07:14.052
I expected Captain Nemo to join us, but he didn't appear.

114
00:07:15.216 --> 00:07:20.401
Ned and Conseil returned to their cabin, and I returned to mine, where I found that my dinner

115
00:07:20.461 --> 00:07:20.881
was ready.

116
00:07:21.962 --> 00:07:25.164
I passed the evening reading, writing, and thinking.

117
00:07:26.165 --> 00:07:32.030
That night I slept deeply while the Nautilus glided rapidly through the current of the Black River.
